Implementation PR: _TBD_ (branch `mkirova/web-lock`)
Champion: @MayaKirova 

## Background and Motivation

`Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` exposes standard browser APIs to Blazor as typed .NET projections reached from `IBrowserPlatform.Window`, such as `Window.LocalStorage`, `Window.CookieStore`, and `Window.Navigator`. #69876 asks for the [Web Locks API](https://w3c.github.io/web-locks/), which coordinates work across the tabs, windows, and workers of the same origin. Examples are electing one tab to own a connection, or keeping two tabs from syncing the same IndexedDB data at the same time.

`navigator.locks.request()` is the only way to take a lock. The browser holds the lock while the promise returned by a callback is pending. So this family also needs the package's first **callback from JavaScript to .NET that is not an event**. This proposal includes a design for such callbacks (see "Callbacks from JavaScript to .NET" below), and Web Locks is its first user.

The projection follows the Web IDL one-to-one, as the other families in the package do:

- `navigator.locks` becomes `Window.Navigator.Locks`, a synchronous property. It is a `[SecureContext]` attribute that returns the same `LockManager` for the life of the page.
- `request()` becomes `RequestAsync()`. The `LockGrantedCallback` becomes `Func<Lock?, Task>`, and the browser holds the lock until the returned `Task` completes.
- `query()` becomes `QueryAsync()`.
- The `Lock` interface, which has only two attributes, becomes a sealed class that the callback receives as a copy.
- The `LockOptions` dictionary becomes a sealed class with `{ get; set; }` properties and the Web IDL defaults. `LockManagerSnapshot` and `LockInfo`, which only the browser returns, become sealed classes with `{ get; init; }` properties, and all their members are nullable, because Web IDL marks none of them `required`.
- The `LockMode` Web IDL enum becomes a `readonly struct` that wraps the string, like `OrientationType`. A value that a newer browser adds still reaches the application.

### Web IDL coverage

| Web IDL | Decision | Reason |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `Navigator.locks` (`NavigatorLocks` mixin) | Implement | Entry point: `Window.Navigator.Locks`. |
| `WorkerNavigator.locks` | Exclude | Blazor code does not run in a worker global scope. |
| `LockManager.request(name, callback)` | Implement | `RequestAsync(string, Func<Lock?, Task>)` |
| `LockManager.request(name, options, callback)` | Implement | `RequestAsync(string, LockOptions, Func<Lock?, Task>)` |
| `LockManager.query()` | Implement | `QueryAsync()` |
| `callback LockGrantedCallback` | Implement | `Func<Lock?, Task>` |
| `interface Lock` | Implement | Copied into a sealed class. |
| `dictionary LockOptions`: `mode`, `ifAvailable`, `steal` | Implement | |
| `dictionary LockOptions`: `signal` | Defer | Waits for the package's `AbortSignal` mapping (#70045, or #70328 / #70826). A `CancellationToken` overload can be added without a breaking change. |
| `dictionary LockManagerSnapshot` | Implement | Returned only. |
| `dictionary LockInfo` | Implement | Returned only. |
| `enum LockMode` | Implement | `readonly struct LockMode`. |

Every member is Baseline "Widely available" (since March 2022), so none is `[Experimental]`.

The browser checks option values, so .NET does not repeat those checks. For example, `steal` together with `ifAvailable`, `steal` with `"shared"`, and a name starting with `-` all reject with `NotSupportedError`. .NET checks only `null` arguments.

## Usage Examples

```csharp
// Program.cs
builder.Services.AddBrowserPlatform();
```

```razor
@using Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform
@inject IBrowserPlatform Browser

@code {
    // Run the sync in one tab at a time. Other tabs wait for the lock.
    private async Task SyncAsync()
    {
        await Browser.Window.Navigator.Locks.RequestAsync("sync-cache", async lock =>
        {
            await SyncCacheAsync();
        }); // The lock is released when the callback's Task completes.
    }

    // Leader election: take the lock only if no other tab holds it.
    private async Task TryBecomeLeaderAsync()
    {
        await Browser.Window.Navigator.Locks.RequestAsync(
            "leader",
            new LockOptions { IfAvailable = true },
            async lock =>
            {
                if (lock is null)
                {
                    status = "Another tab is the leader.";
                    return;
                }

                await RunAsLeaderAsync(); // Holds the lock until this completes.
            });
    }

    // Several readers at once.
    private Task ReadAsync()
        => Browser.Window.Navigator.Locks.RequestAsync(
            "inventory",
            new LockOptions { Mode = LockMode.Shared },
            async lock => await ReadInventoryAsync()).AsTask();

    // Inspect the locks of the origin.
    private async Task ShowLocksAsync()
    {
        var snapshot = await Browser.Window.Navigator.Locks.QueryAsync();

        foreach (var info in snapshot.Held ?? [])
        {
            Console.WriteLine($"{info.Name} ({info.Mode}) held by {info.ClientId}");
        }
    }
}
```

## Proposed API

```diff
+namespace Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform;
+
+public static class NavigatorLockManagerExtensions
+{
+    extension(Navigator navigator)
+    {
+        public LockManager Locks { get; }
+    }
+}
+
+public sealed class LockManager
+{
+    public ValueTask RequestAsync(string name, Func<Lock?, Task> callback);
+    public ValueTask RequestAsync(string name, LockOptions options, Func<Lock?, Task> callback);
+    public ValueTask<LockManagerSnapshot> QueryAsync();
+}
+
+public sealed class Lock
+{
+    public Lock();
+    public string Name { get; init; }
+    public LockMode Mode { get; init; }
+}
+
+public sealed class LockOptions
+{
+    public LockOptions();
+    public LockMode Mode { get; set; } = LockMode.Exclusive;
+    public bool IfAvailable { get; set; }
+    public bool Steal { get; set; }
+}
+
+public sealed class LockManagerSnapshot
+{
+    public LockManagerSnapshot();
+    public ImmutableArray<LockInfo>? Held { get; init; }
+    public ImmutableArray<LockInfo>? Pending { get; init; }
+}
+
+public sealed class LockInfo
+{
+    public LockInfo();
+    public string? Name { get; init; }
+    public LockMode? Mode { get; init; }
+    public string? ClientId { get; init; }
+}
+
+[JsonConverter(typeof(WebIdlEnumJsonConverter<LockMode>))]
+public readonly struct LockMode : IEquatable<LockMode>
+{
+    public LockMode(string value);
+    public static LockMode Shared { get; }
+    public static LockMode Exclusive { get; }
+    public string Value { get; }
+    public override string ToString();
+    public bool Equals(LockMode other);
+    public override bool Equals(object? obj);
+    public override int GetHashCode();
+    public static bool operator ==(LockMode left, LockMode right);
+    public static bool operator !=(LockMode left, LockMode right);
+}
```

Behavior:

- `LockManager` is shared within the scope and cannot be disposed by application code, like `Storage`, `CookieStore`, and `Navigator`.
- Every member throws `NotSupportedException` when the page has no `navigator.locks`, for example on an insecure (HTTP) page. No callback is created in that case.
- `RequestAsync` completes after the lock is released. The callback receives `null` only when `IfAvailable` is `true` and the lock was not granted.
- An exception thrown by the callback releases the lock and is thrown from `RequestAsync` as it is, not wrapped in a JS interop exception.
- `default(LockMode).Value` throws `InvalidOperationException`, and `ToString()` returns `""`. No single Web IDL default covers every use of the enum. `LockOptions.mode` defaults to `"exclusive"`, but `LockInfo.mode` and `Lock.mode` have no default. A `LockOptions` whose `Mode` is `default` throws `InvalidOperationException` before anything is sent.

## Callbacks from JavaScript to .NET

Events already cross from JavaScript to .NET through an internal bridge. `JSCallbackFactory` creates a JS function from the package's module (`createCallback` in `js/Platform.ts`) that calls a `[JSInvokable]` .NET object. An `ArgumentShape` list says which properties of each argument to copy, so a browser object such as an event or a `Lock` becomes a plain .NET object, and no JS object reference is created. The promise that the JS function returns settles when the .NET `Task` completes. For Web Locks, that is exactly what holds the lock.

This proposal reuses that bridge and adds no JavaScript. It separates callbacks into two kinds with different lifetimes:

| | Event-like callback | Operation callback (new) |
|---|---|---|
| Examples | events; later `ResizeObserver`, `watchPosition` | `locks.request()`; later `geolocation.getCurrentPosition()` |
| Calls | 0..N, until removed | at most once, during one operation |
| Ended by | disposing the result, or canceling a token | the operation settling |
| Does JS wait for the .NET `Task`? | no | yes |
| Exception from the .NET callback | logged; the callback stays | rejects the promise, and is thrown from the operation's method |
| Internal type | `EventListener` / `EventListenerCollection` | `JSCallbackFactory.InvokeWithCallbackAsync` |

Callbacks whose return value JavaScript uses synchronously, such as a `NodeFilter`, cannot be projected, because calls from JavaScript into .NET are always asynchronous.

`InvokeWithCallbackAsync` creates the JS function, passes it to the operation, and releases the function and its `DotNetObjectReference` when the operation settles. When the callback throws, it records the exception and rethrows it, so the browser sees a rejected promise (and releases the lock). When the operation then fails with a `JSException`, it throws the recorded .NET exception instead.

The public shape of a callback is `Func<TArg, Task>`, the same as an event listener. A callback whose result JavaScript uses, `Promise<any>` in Web IDL, would need a `Func<TArg, Task<TResult>>` overload later. Web Locks does not need one, because a .NET caller can capture a local value instead.

## Alternative Designs

- **Generic `RequestAsync<TResult>`.** `request()` resolves with the callback's result. A `Func<Lock?, Task<TResult>>` overload could return it. It is left out because a caller can capture a local value, and it can be added later without a breaking change.
- **`LockOptions.Signal` as a property** instead of a later `CancellationToken` parameter. The package maps `AbortSignal` to a `CancellationToken` parameter, as .NET does.
- **Rename `Lock`**, for example to `WebLock`, to avoid `System.Threading.Lock` (see Risks). Rejected, to keep the Web IDL name that MDN uses. The collision is documented on the type.
- **Ship `QueryAsync` only.** Without `request()`, .NET cannot take a lock, so the family would only observe locks that JavaScript or other tabs hold.
- **Reuse `EventListener` for the callback.** Its contract is different. It logs exceptions instead of propagating them, the caller owns its lifetime, and JavaScript does not wait for it.

## Risks

- **A held lock survives a lost Interactive Server connection.** The JS promise that waits for the .NET callback does not settle when the circuit's connection drops. An E2E check held a lock from an Interactive Server component and took the browser offline. The reconnect UI appeared after about 30 seconds, and the browser still held the lock 60 seconds after the connection was lost. The lock is released when the callback completes after a reconnect, or when the page unloads. Other tabs wait for it meanwhile. WebAssembly is not affected. Fixing it needs either the framework to reject pending calls into .NET when the circuit ends, or the package's JS module to settle callback promises on disconnect. Either change is internal and can come after this API.
- **`Lock` and `System.Threading.Lock`.** `System.Threading` is an implicit using in every SDK project. So a component that imports `Microsoft.AspNetCore.Components.Platform` and names the type `Lock` explicitly gets CS0104. A lambda such as `async lock => ...` infers the type and is not affected. The XML docs of `Lock` describe the alias workaround.
- **Long-held locks.** A lock held for the life of a component, as in leader election, keeps a JS function and a `DotNetObjectReference` until the request settles or the scope ends.

## Source justifications

- Scope: #69876: "Expose the Web Locks API for coordinating work across tabs and browser contexts." and "Browser.Navigator.Locks … LockManager, Lock, LockOptions, LockManagerSnapshot, LockInfo, Lock modes, Lock-granted callback support"
- IDL: webref `ed/idl/web-locks.idl`: `[SecureContext] interface mixin NavigatorLocks { readonly attribute LockManager locks; }`, `Promise<any> request(DOMString name, LockGrantedCallback callback);`, `callback LockGrantedCallback = Promise<any> (Lock? lock);`, `dictionary LockOptions { LockMode mode = "exclusive"; boolean ifAvailable = false; boolean steal = false; AbortSignal signal; }`
- Request semantics: Web Locks spec §3.2.1: the promise "resolves (or rejects) with the result of the callback after the lock is released, or rejects if the request is aborted"
- Baseline: MDN Web Locks API: "Baseline Widely available … since March 2022"
- Callback bridge: `src/Components/Platform/src/js/Platform.ts`: "The returned promise settles when the .NET method completes."
- Cancellation: #70045 ("Add AbortLink to cancel browser operations with a CancellationToken"), #70328, #70826
- Implementation: `src/Components/Platform/src/PublicAPI.Unshipped.txt`, `LockManager.cs`, `JSCallbackFactory.InvokeWithCallbackAsync`, `test/LockManagerTest.cs`, `PlatformTestApp.E2E.Tests/Tests/LockManagerTests.cs`

